## How to decide

Pushback is not proof that the finding was wrong. People push back on correct
findings all the time: they fixed it differently, they accept the risk, the
platform does not allow the fix, or it belongs in another PR. None of those is
`premise_wrong`, and none of them may become a rule.

Pick `premise_wrong` only when the reply or the code shows the problem never
existed in the code you commented on — for example the value is validated by
the caller, the "missing" handling is elsewhere and was already there, or the
behaviour is a deliberate, documented contract. If the code near the finding
changed after you raised it, or the thread was about whether a fix landed, the
finding was most likely right: pick `fixed`.

When you pick `premise_wrong`, write `rule` for future reviews of other code.
Bad: "Do not flag readOrderCascade in convex/orders.ts." Good: "Do not flag a
missing tenant check on a Convex query whose handler already calls
requireTenant(ctx) before reading." Use `repo` or `language` scope only when
the reply describes a convention that is clearly broader than this file.

